.TH CCOL_ITER_DECLARE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_iter_declare \- declare a typed iterator variable with RAII cleanup
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

ccol_iter_declare(container, it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a cmap_iterator pointer with the name it. That pointer
carries the type information of container. The declaration adds
__attribute__((cleanup)), so the iterator is destroyed automatically at the
end of its scope. The caller needs no explicit ccol_iter_destroy() call.

The macro also declares two hidden companion type variables. Those
variables let ccol_iter_key_ptr() and ccol_iter_val_ptr() give pointers
with the correct type for container.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
container  Container variable. The caller must declare it first with a
           *_declare or *_construct macro. It must be a cvec, a chmap or
           a cbmap.
it         Name for the iterator variable.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
container must be in scope. Its companion type variables must also be
visible, in the same scope or in a parent scope.
This macro needs GCC or Clang. It uses __attribute__((cleanup)).
A loop that leaves early leaves the iterator live until the end of the
scope. The container can be destroyed before that point: the cleanup
frees the iterator without reading the container. Do not advance or
dereference such an iterator once its container is gone.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_iter_declare(my_map, it);
for (it = ccol_begin(my_map); it != NULL; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%d\en", *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_for_each (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3)
